From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Almeida Barrios <gabarrios2006@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:51:32 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Hand each group participant's timed PCM to a sink that mixes
 senders itself

In a group call every participant's decoded PCM went through the
participant mixer: per-sender queues with a 120 ms prefill, drop-oldest
past 240 ms, a 10 ms wall-clock ticker, and one untimed stream to the sink,
whose own reservoir then buffered it again. The timed NetEq playout of
direct calls (TimedAudioSink) never applied to groups.

A sink can now implement MixedTimedAudioSink: when it wants mixed timed
audio, each participant frame is written with that participant's SSRC and
PCM-unit RTP timestamp, so the sink runs one NetEq per sender on the device
clock and mixes them, as WebRTC does. Concealed frames are not sent to it
(its NetEq conceals per sender); recovered frames are. The roster still
decides who is heard. The mixer ticker drops whatever was waiting while
such a sink is attached, so none of it arrives later as untimed PCM. Every
other sink, and this one when it stops wanting mixed timed audio, keeps the
mixer exactly as before.

Tested: frames reach the sink on each sender's SSRC/timeline and not the
mixer; sinks without the capability (protocol 2, no NetEq, plain sinks)
keep the mixer; the roster filters timed audio; concealment is dropped only
where the sink conceals; mixer reset keeps the roster; full suite passes.
